\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE DAG topology $\mathcal{T}$, gold-label availability $G \in \{\textsc{yes}, \textsc{no}\}$
\ENSURE Recommended method $M$, risk level $R$
\STATE
\STATE \textbf{Step 1: Assess risk level}
\IF{$\mathcal{T} \in \{\text{M-bias}\}$}
    \STATE $R \gets \textsc{Low}$ \COMMENT{$|\text{bias}| < 0.04$ for all $C$}
\ELSIF{$\mathcal{T} \in \{\text{Conf., Med., Coll., Front-door}\}$}
    \STATE $R \gets \textsc{Medium}$ \COMMENT{bounded bias, no sign reversal}
\ELSE
    \STATE $R \gets \textsc{High}$ \COMMENT{Exposure, IV: sign reversal 83.4\% / 52.6\%}
\ENDIF
\STATE
\STATE \textbf{Step 2: Select correction method}
\IF{$G = \textsc{yes}$}
    \IF{$\mathcal{T} = \text{M-bias}$}
        \STATE $M \gets \textsc{None}$ \COMMENT{PPI++ RMSE red.\ $-$88.0\%; no correction preferred}
    \ELSE
        \STATE $M \gets \text{PPI++}$ \COMMENT{47.0\% RMSE red., 96.8\% coverage}
    \ENDIF
\ELSE
    \STATE \COMMENT{no gold labels}
    \IF{$\mathcal{T} \in \{\text{Confounding, Mediation}\}$}
        \STATE $M \gets \text{DSL}$ \COMMENT{48.0\% / 52.9\% RMSE reduction}
    \ELSIF{$\mathcal{T} = \text{IV}$}
        \STATE $M \gets \text{MC-SIMEX}$ \COMMENT{59.4\% RMSE reduction}
    \ELSIF{$\mathcal{T} \in \{\text{Collider, M-bias}\}$}
        \STATE $M \gets \text{MC-SIMEX}$ \COMMENT{31.8\% / 0.1\% RMSE reduction}
    \ELSE
        \STATE $M \gets \emptyset$; flag: acquire gold labels \COMMENT{no reliable gold-free method}
    \ENDIF
\ENDIF
\STATE
\STATE \textbf{Step 3: FWL invariance warning}
\IF{$\mathcal{T} \in \{\text{Conf., Med., Coll., M-bias}\}$}
    \STATE Skip Naive plug-in and MLA \COMMENT{0\% bias red.\ on $T$ coeff.}
\ENDIF
\RETURN $(M, R)$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Proof of FWL Invariance}
\label{app:fwl_proof}

For the four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias), the regression is $Y = \tau T + \boldsymbol{\gamma}^\top \mathbf{D}^* + \varepsilon$, where $\mathbf{D}^*$ is the $N \times (K{-}1)$ dummy matrix for $A^*$. Any matrix-based correction replaces $\mathbf{D}^*$ with $\tilde{\mathbf{D}} = \mathbf{D}^* M$ for some invertible $(K{-}1) \times (K{-}1)$ matrix $M$ (e.g., $M = C^{-1}$ for plug-in, or $M = (C^\top C)^{-1} C^\top$ for MLE). By the Frisch--Waugh--Lovell theorem~\cite{lovell1963}, $\hat{\tau}$ depends only on the residual of $T$ projected off $\operatorname{col}(\mathbf{D}^*)$. Since $\operatorname{col}(\mathbf{D}^* M) = \operatorname{col}(\mathbf{D}^*)$ for invertible $M$, the projection is unchanged and $\hat{\tau}(\tilde{\mathbf{D}}) = \hat{\tau}(\mathbf{D}^*)$ exactly. SIMEX adds noise $\mathbf{D}^*_{\text{sim}} = \mathbf{D}^* + \lambda \mathbf{E}$; when $\mathbf{E}$ is generated from the same column space, the extrapolation to $\lambda = -1$ converges to a column-space-equivalent transformation, yielding 0\% bias reduction empirically.

\subsection{Applied Illustration: ClinicalBERT Case Study}
\label{app:case_study}

To demonstrate the framework's end-to-end utility, we apply it retrospectively to \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024}, who use ClinicalBERT to classify patient smoking status ($K{=}4$: never, past, current, unknown) from clinical notes and control for this predicted confounder when estimating the causal effect of echocardiography on mortality in 4{,}735 MIMIC-III sepsis patients.
Our taxonomy identifies this as a \emph{confounding topology}: the annotated variable enters as a confounder between treatment and outcome.
Three conclusions follow directly.
First, the zero sign-flip guarantee for confounding (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}) ensures that the estimated protective effect (OR~$= 0.89$) cannot reverse direction due to annotation error---a reassurance unavailable without topology-specific analysis.
Second, the FWL invariance result implies that any correction reducing to $C^{-1}$ applied to the dummy-coded confounder achieves exactly 0\% bias reduction for the treatment coefficient; naive plug-in is provably futile here.
Third, our correction guidance ranks PPI++ (83.2\% RMSE reduction for confounding) above MC-SIMEX (35.1\%) when gold labels are available---a concrete recommendation the original study lacked.
This example illustrates the intended workflow: DAG identification $\to$ topology-specific guarantees $\to$ correction method selection.

\subsection{IV Wald Estimator: Diagonal-Dominance Subspaces}
\label{sec:iv_dirichlet_subspace}

Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy_results} reports a 52.6\% Wald sign-flip rate
over the full Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior. Because realistic LLM confusion
matrices are diagonal-dominant, we replicate the exposure-DAG stratification
for the IV topology, restricting the sample
space to $\{C : \min_k C_{kk} \geq d\}$ for $d \in \{0, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9\}$
(100{,}000 random matrices per threshold).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IV Wald estimator bias by minimum-diagonal subspace ($K{=}3$, $\tau_{\text{true}}{=}1.41$).
Rates over 100{,}000 Dirichlet-truncated random $C$ per threshold.}
\label{tab:iv_dirichlet_subspace}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subspace} & \textbf{Violation} & \textbf{Sign Flip} & \textbf{Amplif.} & \textbf{Worst} \\
\midrule
Full           & 100.0\% & 51.7\% & 48.3\% & $10^{5}\!\times$ \\
$d \geq 0.5$   & 100.0\% & 0.0\%  & 100.0\% & $28.6\times$ \\
$d \geq 0.7$   & 100.0\% & 0.0\%  & 100.0\% & $2.4\times$ \\
$d \geq 0.9$   & 100.0\% & 0.0\%  & 100.0\% & $1.2\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The contrast with exposure is instructive: at $d{\geq}0.7$, exposure violation drops to 13.7\% (sign reversal 2.1\%, amplification 11.7\%), while IV maintains 100\% violation with pure amplification. IV sign reversal is entirely driven by pathological (near-random) confusion matrices where the Wald denominator crosses zero; diagonal dominance prevents this crossing but cannot prevent amplification. Even at $d{\geq}0.9$, the Wald estimator inflates $|\tau|$ by up to $1.24\times$ with probability~1.

\subsection{Non-Differential Assumption Stress Test}
\label{sec:nondiff_stress}

Our main analysis assumes non-differential misclassification: the confusion matrix $C$ does not depend on the outcome $Y$. To assess sensitivity to violations of this assumption, we conduct a stress test on the exposure topology ($K{=}3$, $\min_k C_{kk} \geq 0.7$, 10{,}000 random matrices per $\varepsilon$). For each base matrix $C_0$, observations with $Y > \text{median}(Y)$ are misclassified using a perturbed matrix $\tilde{C} = \text{renormalize}(C_0 + \varepsilon \cdot \Delta)$ where $\Delta_{jk} \sim \text{Uniform}(-1,1)$ for $j \neq k$, while observations with $Y \leq \text{median}(Y)$ retain $C_0$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Effect of differential misclassification on bias violation rates (exposure DAG, $K{=}3$, $\min_k C_{kk} \geq 0.7$, $N{=}50{,}000$ MC samples per trial).}
\label{tab:nondiff_stress}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
$\varepsilon$ & Sign-Flip (\%) & Amplification (\%) & Any Violation (\%) \\
\midrule
0.00 & 2.0 & 11.5 & 13.3 \\
0.01 & 2.4 & 12.4 & 14.7 \\
0.05 & 2.8 & 13.0 & 15.7 \\
0.10 & 4.5 & 16.7 & 20.9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The $\varepsilon{=}0$ baseline (13.3\% violation) closely matches the analytical result (13.7\%) from Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy_results}. Even at $\varepsilon{=}0.10$---a substantial departure from non-differential error---the violation rate increases by only 7.6 percentage points. Small departures ($\varepsilon \leq 0.05$) change rates by fewer than 3 percentage points, suggesting that our main results are moderately robust to mild violations of the non-differential assumption.

\paragraph{Extended stress test.}
An extended stress test across 96 configurations (4 topologies $\times$ 2 $K$ $\times$ 3 CM qualities $\times$ 4 $\varepsilon$, 100 MC replications each; Table~\ref{tab:stress-test}) confirms these patterns broadly: at $\varepsilon{=}0$, sign-flip rates are zero for all configurations; at $\varepsilon{=}0.10$, the maximum sign-flip rate is 7\% (IV, low-quality $C_{kk}{=}0.4$, $K{=}3$). At $K{=}10$, sign-flip rates remain zero for all topologies and $\varepsilon$ values, suggesting that higher $K$ provides additional robustness to differential misclassification.

